58 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (Global Navigation Satellite Systems \(GNSS\) have become critical to the functioning of our modern technology-based societies through Positioning, Navigation and Timing \(PNT\). These services drive civilian and military applications ranging from smartphones navigation and fleet management to aviation essential systems, maritime navigation, as well as controlling autonomous unmanned platforms. Because critical and safety-relevant applications are increasingly based on GNSS there is a growing need to achieve integrity and reliability of satellite-based navigation. Finally, GNSS signals, while being a great technology to have offer very low-powered signals once they reach ground level and are susceptible. Spoofing attacks are especially harmful, )
/E (Global Navigation Satellite Systems \(GNSS\) have become critical to the functioning of our modern technology-based societies through Positioning, Navigation and Timing \(PNT\). These services drive civilian and military applications ranging from smartphones navigation and fleet management to aviation essential systems, maritime navigation, as well as controlling autonomous unmanned platforms. Because critical and safety-relevant applications are increasingly based on GNSS there is a growing need to achieve integrity and reliability of satellite-based navigation. Finally, GNSS signals, while being a great technology to have offer very low-powered signals once they reach ground level and are susceptible. Spoofing attacks are especially harmful, )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
59 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 48 0 R
/T (Abstract. Global Navigation Satellite Systems \(GNSS\) have become an indispensable part of our contemporary infrastructure for navigation and timing, being employed in several mission critical applications ranging from unmanned aerial vehicles \(UAVs\), autonomous transportation systems to aviation, maritime navigation and communication networks. Nevertheless, even when there exists such a widespread use, GNSS is still afflicted by weak authenticity of transmitted signals \(spoofing\); that is an attacker could send counterfeit signals, which can deceive the receiver involved in calculating wrong position or velocity or timing information. These attacks represent a great risk especially in safety critical contexts where the non-detection of even an only spoofing is enough to cause catastrophic events.  In this paper, we propose an integrated artificial intelligence-based framework for GNSS spoofing attack detection on real signal data. Three sophisticated machine learning models like Deep Neural Networks \(DNN\), Random Forest \(RF\) and Extreme Gradient Boosting \(XGBoost\) were constructed, trained and tested to see how well they could distinguish genuine and spoofed signals. The achieved cross-sections are based on realistic data sets. )
/E (Abstract. Global Navigation Satellite Systems \(GNSS\) have become an indispensable part of our contemporary infrastructure for navigation and timing, being employed in several mission critical applications ranging from unmanned aerial vehicles \(UAVs\), autonomous transportation systems to aviation, maritime navigation and communication networks. Nevertheless, even when there exists such a widespread use, GNSS is still afflicted by weak authenticity of transmitted signals \(spoofing\); that is an attacker could send counterfeit signals, which can deceive the receiver involved in calculating wrong position or velocity or timing information. These attacks represent a great risk especially in safety critical contexts where the non-detection of even an only spoofing is enough to cause catastrophic events.  In this paper, we propose an integrated artificial intelligence-based framework for GNSS spoofing attack detection on real signal data. Three sophisticated machine learning models like Deep Neural Networks \(DNN\), Random Forest \(RF\) and Extreme Gradient Boosting \(XGBoost\) were constructed, trained and tested to see how well they could distinguish genuine and spoofed signals. The achieved cross-sections are based on realistic data sets. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
