/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 49 0 R
/E (This research is oriented towards building, developing and testing as an Artificial intelligence-based framework of the essential points necessary for the operational reliability in applied real-life experiences and in real time regarding the performance of spoofing attacks on Global Navigation Satellite System \(GNSS\) applications. This work aims to overcome the classic spoofing detection approach limitation by using advanced learning machine models, which can learn more complex signal behavior in nominal and spoofed environments. The motivation behind this work is the recent growth of GNSS dependency in safety-critical applications such as unmanned aerial vehicles, autonomous navigation systems and timing-critical infrastructures, where even a single undetected spoofing attack can lead to catastrophic operational and security consequences. Therefore, the goal is not just to maximize classification accuracy, but also to minimize false-negative errors for reliable and timely detection of spoofing attacks across varying environmental conditions. In order to fulfil this aim, the following specific objectives are set: )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
