/T (Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) and Machine Learning \(ML\) have proven to be essential tools for identifying GNSS spoofing; they can be applied in actual situations where hybrid datasets produce a more accurate result than using conventional simulation techniques. Before developing their proposed research methodologies, the researchers produced a detailed plan outlining how they would implement it. Their plan consisted of: \(i\) identifying what data they would need and how they would collect it; \(ii\) using a method that used characteristics of GNSS Spoof and/or MLI in the feature engineering process; \(iii\) making train datasets for all the models tested \(1-1-DNN, RF, and XGBoost\), evaluating performance for each, and \(iv\) conducting a comparative analysis to compare results between models "apples to apples." The results of the experiment demonstrated that XGBoost had the most success overall, classifying the true GNSS Signal from the Spoofed GNSS Signal with an average classification rate above 93% across all three methods. These findings confirm that XGBoost is the most successful model in classifying GNSS Signals; it has the fewest false negatives, the highest recall rate \(for classifying true GNSS Signals versus spoofed GNSS Signals\), and the highest degree of classifying GNSS Signals for high-risk GNSS Navigation Systems. We found in this study that the integration of artificial intelligence \(AI\) into Global Navigation Satellite System \(GNSS\) based systems will serve to enhance both Navigation Security and Reliable GNSS Navigation in today's environment. The outcomes of this study also illustrate that the assessment of Spoofing Detection Systems for Safety Critical Applications must include a more comprehensive set of evaluation metrics than simply accuracy. This is because metrics such as recall and reducing the number of false negatives offer a more  realistic means to evaluate Spoofing Detection Systems in Safety Critical  Applications. Furthermore, this study has provided a robust scientific and practical basis for the construction of smart and Real-Time Deployable GNSS Spoofing Detection Systems. Therefore, the results of this study will have tremendous benefits for the Satellite Navigation Security field. The next step is to further research the integration of this work into real-world Application Platforms such as: Unmanned Aerial Vehicles, Autonomous Vehicles, and Timing- and Location-Critical Infrastructure. )
/E (Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) and Machine Learning \(ML\) have proven to be essential tools for identifying GNSS spoofing; they can be applied in actual situations where hybrid datasets produce a more accurate result than using conventional simulation techniques. Before developing their proposed research methodologies, the researchers produced a detailed plan outlining how they would implement it. Their plan consisted of: \(i\) identifying what data they would need and how they would collect it; \(ii\) using a method that used characteristics of GNSS Spoof and/or MLI in the feature engineering process; \(iii\) making train datasets for all the models tested \(1-1-DNN, RF, and XGBoost\), evaluating performance for each, and \(iv\) conducting a comparative analysis to compare results between models "apples to apples." The results of the experiment demonstrated that XGBoost had the most success overall, classifying the true GNSS Signal from the Spoofed GNSS Signal with an average classification rate above 93% across all three methods. These findings confirm that XGBoost is the most successful model in classifying GNSS Signals; it has the fewest false negatives, the highest recall rate \(for classifying true GNSS Signals versus spoofed GNSS Signals\), and the highest degree of classifying GNSS Signals for high-risk GNSS Navigation Systems. We found in this study that the integration of artificial intelligence \(AI\) into Global Navigation Satellite System \(GNSS\) based systems will serve to enhance both Navigation Security and Reliable GNSS Navigation in today's environment. The outcomes of this study also illustrate that the assessment of Spoofing Detection Systems for Safety Critical Applications must include a more comprehensive set of evaluation metrics than simply accuracy. This is because metrics such as recall and reducing the number of false negatives offer a more  realistic means to evaluate Spoofing Detection Systems in Safety Critical  Applications. Furthermore, this study has provided a robust scientific and practical basis for the construction of smart and Real-Time Deployable GNSS Spoofing Detection Systems. Therefore, the results of this study will have tremendous benefits for the Satellite Navigation Security field. The next step is to further research the integration of this work into real-world Application Platforms such as: Unmanned Aerial Vehicles, Autonomous Vehicles, and Timing- and Location-Critical Infrastructure. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
