263 0 obj
<<
/Title (Abstract. Global Navigation Satellite Systems \(GNSS\) have become an indispensable part of our contemporary infrastructure for navigation and timing, being employed in several mission critical applications ranging from unmanned aerial vehicles \(UAVs\), autonomous transportation systems to aviation, maritime navigation and communication networks. Nevertheless, even when there exists such a widespread use, GNSS is still afflicted by weak authenticity of transmitted signals \(spoofing\); that is an attacker could send counterfeit signals, which can deceive the receiver involved in calculating wrong position or velocity or timing information. These attacks represent a great risk especially in safety critical contexts where the non-detection of even an only spoofing is enough to cause catastrophic events.  In this paper, we propose an integrated artificial intelligence-based framework for GNSS spoofing attack detection on real signal data. Three sophisticated machine learning models like Deep Neural Networks \(DNN\), Random Forest \(RF\) and Extreme Gradient Boosting \(XGBoost\) were constructed, trained and tested to see how well they could distinguish genuine and spoofed signals. The achieved cross-sections are based on realistic data sets.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 182 0 R
/Prev 262 0 R
/Next 229 0 R
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Title (discovering an analyzing GNSS jamming and spoofing capabilities, as well as from Operational GNSS user receivers \(i.e., GNSS Support Systems, such as NavIC; GPS, etc.\). Each data sample was assigned a binary classification of Authentic or Spoofed, allowing for the supervised technique of learning to be applied [3], [4].  The datasets include multiple GNSS signal attributes including, but not limited to;)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 231 0 R
